\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {25}{\ignorespaces Constants of the minor operations of each stage of the segmentation block, with the reason for each. Every entry in units of the measured pitch $P$ scales with the page; the four that do not are the Otsu range, the $0.2\,\%$ and $0.45$ paper-region tests and the floor of 60 ink pixels. The constants of the core operations appear in the equations of Section~\ref {sec:segmentation} itself.}}{109}{table.caption.83}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:seg-const}{{25}{109}{Constants of the minor operations of each stage of the segmentation block, with the reason for each. Every entry in units of the measured pitch $P$ scales with the page; the four that do not are the Otsu range, the $0.2\,\%$ and $0.45$ paper-region tests and the floor of 60 ink pixels. The constants of the core operations appear in the equations of Section~\ref {sec:segmentation} itself}{table.caption.83}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {26}{\ignorespaces The eight discard predicates applied to each closed piece of ink in stage~4; the background label is exempt from all of them. The first row is the most aggressive rule in the module: any piece whose leftmost ink lies within $e$ of the left frame edge is discarded whatever its size or darkness, which removes the facing page of an open book but also costs the first letters of a page photographed with no left margin.}}{110}{table.caption.85}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:seg-artifact}{{26}{110}{The eight discard predicates applied to each closed piece of ink in stage~4; the background label is exempt from all of them. The first row is the most aggressive rule in the module: any piece whose leftmost ink lies within $e$ of the left frame edge is discarded whatever its size or darkness, which removes the facing page of an open book but also costs the first letters of a page photographed with no left margin}{table.caption.85}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {27}{\ignorespaces The pixel-ownership correction rules of stage~6. The size ladder they share runs $0.006P^2$, $0.01P^2$, $0.015P^2$, $0.03P^2$, $0.05P^2$, $0.065P^2$, $0.1P^2$, $0.12P^2$ and $0.2P^2$, every entry a multiple of the measured pitch squared, so no rule is tied to a pixel count.}}{110}{table.caption.87}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:seg-own}{{27}{110}{The pixel-ownership correction rules of stage~6. The size ladder they share runs $0.006P^2$, $0.01P^2$, $0.015P^2$, $0.03P^2$, $0.05P^2$, $0.065P^2$, $0.1P^2$, $0.12P^2$ and $0.2P^2$, every entry a multiple of the measured pitch squared, so no rule is tied to a pixel count}{table.caption.87}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsubsection}{Pseudocode of the four routines that carry the arithmetic}{110}{subsubsection*.88}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {25}{\ignorespaces The three array primitives of the segmentation block as pseudocode. \texttt  {BoxMean} is Equation~\ref {eq:seg-sat}, \texttt  {AnyWithinRadius} is Equation~\ref {eq:seg-dilate} and \texttt  {LongRunMask} is Equation~\ref {eq:seg-runmask}. Because each is built on a cumulative sum, a window of any size costs four look-ups, which is what makes the 40-pixel illumination blur of stage~2 affordable.}}{111}{figure.caption.89}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:seg-primspseudo}{{25}{111}{The three array primitives of the segmentation block as pseudocode. \texttt {BoxMean} is Equation~\ref {eq:seg-sat}, \texttt {AnyWithinRadius} is Equation~\ref {eq:seg-dilate} and \texttt {LongRunMask} is Equation~\ref {eq:seg-runmask}. Because each is built on a cumulative sum, a window of any size costs four look-ups, which is what makes the 40-pixel illumination blur of stage~2 affordable}{figure.caption.89}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {26}{\ignorespaces Lighting normalisation and the ink mask as pseudocode. The array \texttt  {ratio} is $\rho $ of Equation~\ref {eq:seg-flat} and \texttt  {OtsuThreshold} is the cumulative form of Equation~\ref {eq:seg-otsu} evaluated for all 256 bins at once. The threshold 0.999 rather than 1 absorbs floating-point error in the integral image; no pen stroke fills a $9\times 9$ window, so a window that is wholly ink is a shadow or a binder and is removed with its fringe.}}{111}{figure.caption.90}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:seg-inkpseudo}{{26}{111}{Lighting normalisation and the ink mask as pseudocode. The array \texttt {ratio} is $\rho $ of Equation~\ref {eq:seg-flat} and \texttt {OtsuThreshold} is the cumulative form of Equation~\ref {eq:seg-otsu} evaluated for all 256 bins at once. The threshold 0.999 rather than 1 absorbs floating-point error in the integral image; no pen stroke fills a $9\times 9$ window, so a window that is wholly ink is a shadow or a binder and is removed with its fringe}{figure.caption.90}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{Supporting detail of the text recogniser}{111}{subsection*.93}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:app-rec}{{6.4}{111}{Supporting detail of the text recogniser}{subsection*.93}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {27}{\ignorespaces The pitch measurement as pseudocode. The peak test is Equation~\ref {eq:seg-centres} and the median gap is the pitch $P$ of Equation~\ref {eq:seg-pitch}. The smoothed profile is returned as well and the caller reuses it for the line centres, so the scale unit and the line positions come out of one pass over the mask. The autocorrelation is a fallback only because it can lock onto a multiple of the true spacing.}}{112}{figure.caption.91}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:seg-pitchpseudo}{{27}{112}{The pitch measurement as pseudocode. The peak test is Equation~\ref {eq:seg-centres} and the median gap is the pitch $P$ of Equation~\ref {eq:seg-pitch}. The smoothed profile is returned as well and the caller reuses it for the line centres, so the scale unit and the line positions come out of one pass over the mask. The autocorrelation is a fallback only because it can lock onto a multiple of the true spacing}{figure.caption.91}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {28}{\ignorespaces The least-ink seam as pseudocode. The cost field is Equation~\ref {eq:seg-cost2} and the loop over columns is the recursion of Equation~\ref {eq:seg-seam}. All rows of a column are evaluated at once as $2s+1$ shifted candidate arrays, so only the displacements and the backtrack are loops. At a weight of $4.0$ against a maximum of $0.012$, crossing one ink pixel costs over three hundred times more than sitting a half-gap from the midline, so the midline term only breaks ties over the gaps between words.}}{112}{figure.caption.92}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:seg-seampseudo}{{28}{112}{The least-ink seam as pseudocode. The cost field is Equation~\ref {eq:seg-cost2} and the loop over columns is the recursion of Equation~\ref {eq:seg-seam}. All rows of a column are evaluated at once as $2s+1$ shifted candidate arrays, so only the displacements and the backtrack are loops. At a weight of $4.0$ against a maximum of $0.012$, crossing one ink pixel costs over three hundred times more than sitting a half-gap from the midline, so the midline term only breaks ties over the gaps between words}{figure.caption.92}{}}
