\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {34}{\ignorespaces Deployed forward pass of both writer classifiers as pseudocode. The two pools are Equation~\ref {eq:wid-pool} and the two linear layers Equation~\ref {eq:wid-head}; the recurrent layers of the recogniser are absent, because writer identity does not depend on the letter sequence. Taking a maximum over height and a mean over width makes the feature a position-independent bag of local stroke patterns. The weights come from a plain array archive written by the trainer, with no third-party model format parsed.}}{120}{figure.caption.112}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:wid-forward}{{34}{120}{Deployed forward pass of both writer classifiers as pseudocode. The two pools are Equation~\ref {eq:wid-pool} and the two linear layers Equation~\ref {eq:wid-head}; the recurrent layers of the recogniser are absent, because writer identity does not depend on the letter sequence. Taking a maximum over height and a mean over width makes the feature a position-independent bag of local stroke patterns. The weights come from a plain array archive written by the trainer, with no third-party model format parsed}{figure.caption.112}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {35}{\ignorespaces Stroke normalisation as pseudocode. The band is the core band of Equation~\ref {eq:wid-xh}, the dense band of the lower-case bodies; \texttt  {Skeletonize} is the Zhang--Suen thinning~\cite  {zhang1984} and \texttt  {Dilate} the re-inking at the constant width. Because the width is a fixed fraction of the measured x-height, the result is independent of scan resolution: an x-height of 20 pixels gives a three-pixel pen whatever the camera.}}{120}{figure.caption.113}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:wid-snorm}{{35}{120}{Stroke normalisation as pseudocode. The band is the core band of Equation~\ref {eq:wid-xh}, the dense band of the lower-case bodies; \texttt {Skeletonize} is the Zhang--Suen thinning~\cite {zhang1984} and \texttt {Dilate} the re-inking at the constant width. Because the width is a fixed fraction of the measured x-height, the result is independent of scan resolution: an x-height of 20 pixels gives a three-pixel pen whatever the camera}{figure.caption.113}{}}
